          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            defined on the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} category of cobordisms
            whose morphisms are plain cobordisms and diffeomorphisms between these.
          %p
            In a
            %em conformal
            quantum field theory the cobordisms are equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+structure"} conformal structure
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            structure modulo pointwise rescaling):
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+cobordisms"} conformal cobordisms
          %p
            A conformal field theory (CFT) is accordingly a functor on such a richer category of conformal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            for more details.
          %p
            The conformally invariant quantum field theories have fields for whom the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+functions"} correlation functions
            have a specific behaviour accounting for the
            %em conformal dimension
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+bootstrap"} conformal bootstrap
            program: the conformal invariance should be sufficient to classify consistent conformal QFTs directly from the analysis of symmetries, rather than computing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            from some
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
          %p
            Precisely in 2-dimensions is the representation theory of the conformal group exceptionally interesting. In
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \gt 2
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+group"} conformal group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            dimensions, there are additionaly infinitely many local generators of conformal transformations whose commutation relations are given by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Virasoro+algebra"} Virasoro algebra
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+conformal+field+theory"} rational conformal field theory
            case there are complete classification results for the full theories (defined on cobordisms of all genera).
          %p For this reason often in the literature the term “CFT” often implicitly refers to 2d CFT.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            \-dimensional conformal field theories have two major applications:
          %ul
            %li
              %p they describe critical phenomena on surfaces in condensed matter physics;
            %li
              %p
                they are the building blocks used in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %p
            In the former application it is mostly the
            %em local
            behaviour of the CFT that is relevant. This is encoded in
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theoretic
            applications the extension of the local theory to a full representation of the 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+cobordism+category"} conformal cobordism category
            is crucial. This extension is called
            %em
              solving the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sewing+constraints"} sewing constraints
            \.
          %h2#abstract Abstract
          %p In the definition paragraph we will show how to define a conformal field theory using the axiomatic approach of Wightman resp. Osterwalder-Schrader. There are several approaches to axiomatically define conformal field theory, the said approach is not the most “popular” or “elegant” one. There are two reasons to consider the Wightman approach however: If one is already familiar with the Wightman approach, it helps to put conformal field theories into context. The second reason is that several notions often used by physicists can be easily and rigorously defined and explained using this approach. Therefore, it may serve as a bridge between mathematicians and physics literature.
          %h2#2d_cft_on_the_plane 2d CFT on the plane
          %h3#definition_in_terms_of_the_osterwalderschrader_axioms Definition in Terms of the Osterwalder-Schrader Axioms
          %p
            A definition of conformal field theories can be formulated using the appropriate version of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Osterwalder--Schrader+axioms"} Osterwalder–Schrader axioms
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+functions"} correlation functions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+axioms"} Wightman axioms
          %p Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi i
                  %mo ≠
                  %msub
